That's the reason that most of the, uh, um, college football games had every team, but

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:28.100
they had zero names.

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:32.580
It was just numbers and they sort of like looked like the archetype of the player.

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:37.500
Right. Uh, but I thought it was cool to see that and to also get all those stats.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:41.860
Uh, we'll obviously get into the gameplay, but, uh, that was something that just grabbed

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:44.400
me right away is that like, we haven't seen this before.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:48.320
Um, whether, you know, we played almost every type of sport now.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:49.820
I can't think of which ones were missing.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:51.660
Uh, cricket. Yeah, that's true.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:52.680
We didn't get a cricket game yet.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:54.420
Good, good contribution, Sean.

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:56.720
Uh, also. There's a cricket game now, Mike.

00:14:56.860 --> 00:15:01.180
Yep. Uh, no, I just think like, you know, for what it's worth, I'm a big baseball fan.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:02.280
What about you guys?

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:04.760
Uh, I grew up in a big baseball house.

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:08.520
I played it when I was younger and I was, I was a fan, but not like, not a big fan.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:11.420
I, I don't really follow the MLB or anything anymore.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:13.300
And your team is like Yankees?

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:15.820
Uh, my family's a big Red Sox fan.

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:17.140
Red Sox fan. Yeah.

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:18.660
So I couldn't have insulted you higher.

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:25.660
Yeah. I, um, I literally knew nothing about the sport until maybe high school.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:30.380
And then I, while I knew how the sport was played, I didn't care about it until college.

00:15:30.380 --> 00:15:33.400
And then you welcomed me into the Mets fandom.

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:35.840
And now I am a casual viewer.

00:15:36.280 --> 00:15:41.820
Um, and I, you know, I'll watch the world series and I'll, I'll watch the Mets when they're good, but that's been a while.

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:46.180
Yeah. And, uh, as Sean mentioned, I am a New York Mets fan.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:49.040
This is a great time to be a New York Mets fan.

00:15:49.060 --> 00:15:57.520
Uh, in 1988, because even if you're going off the 87 team, we have most of the 86 team, which won the world series in there.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:16:01.340
So, uh, you know, it was, it was not hard to be like, okay, cool.

00:16:01.400 --> 00:16:04.220
I want Gary Carter, Keith Hernandez, Daryl Strawberry.

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:06.040
Like I want those guys in the lineup right away.

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:33.340
So let's just dive into it, right?

00:16:33.520 --> 00:16:34.600
Yeah. All right. Let's do it.

00:16:34.600 --> 00:16:36.440
What happens when you boot up the game, Sean?

00:16:36.780 --> 00:16:48.880
You get to pick if you want to play a one or two player game, then you move on to, uh, whether you would like to play a regular game, a world series game or an all-star game.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:56.060
And the differences, um, uh, amongst those were a little different than what I was expecting, but I guess it makes sense.

00:16:56.260 --> 00:17:02.180
A regular game is just a one-off game between two national league or two American league teams.

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:04.180
You can't go interleague in a regular game?

00:17:04.180 --> 00:17:06.940
You can go interleague. And that's the way it used to be for what it's worth.

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:13.240
Now interleague happens all the time in baseball schedule, but you cannot select a national league team and an American league team.

00:17:13.240 --> 00:17:23.980
That's what the difference was. And that's because in the all-star game, that's when you get to assemble a national league team and select from any player from any team, which just could take forever, honestly.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:25.520
But if you're into that, go for it.

00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:28.460
And then also for the American league, you do the same thing.

00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:31.680
You see, I thought that there was going to be a presentational difference.

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:35.200
I thought that after you won the game, or actually I thought it was going to be a series.

00:17:35.780 --> 00:17:42.080
Right, right. I thought there would be some sort of fanfare or like a graphic of fireworks or something and it just ended.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:47.760
Yeah. I was hoping even the world series one would at least be like a best of seven one, you know?

00:17:47.760 --> 00:17:52.800
And instead it's just, no, now you can pick a national league team versus an American league team.

00:17:52.800 --> 00:17:55.320
And decide if it's going to be DH or not and all that.

00:17:55.420 --> 00:17:56.740
Right, right. Well, yeah.

00:17:57.060 --> 00:18:02.440
So the team selection process, what do we think about that?

00:18:02.940 --> 00:18:04.900
I actually don't remember exactly what's happening.

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:06.540
I remember being a little not intuitive.

00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:07.920
There was something weird about it, right?

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:15.100
Well, the weirdest thing about it for me is that one wrong button and you don't go back one step.

00:18:15.100 --> 00:18:17.180
You go back to the main menu.

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:18.460
I didn't even think I noticed that.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:23.400
And it gives you a really angry beep and just like, you did wrong.

00:18:24.540 --> 00:18:25.900
Try again, dummy.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:32.740
Right. And it's like, there's got to be a reason for like, no, I actually, I didn't, I want to just go back to picking my team again.

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:35.540
Like I don't, it's not even like all the players and stuff.

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:38.920
It's like, I can't even go back to like any previous step.

00:18:38.920 --> 00:18:40.260
I have to start over.

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:43.780
Yeah. So you better make those decisions beforehand.

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:54.000
Yeah. It also doesn't clearly explain that like, you're only allowed to select a certain amount of infielders, outfielders.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.040
Well, yeah. You're also, I mean, you're technically setting your defensive line up.

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:59.060
You're talking about when you're setting the batting order here, right?

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.040
Right. Well, that's the, yeah.

00:19:00.140 --> 00:19:02.300
Yeah. That's the beginning of the game, in my opinion.

00:19:02.500 --> 00:19:03.900
No matter which mode you pick. You're selecting a team.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:05.040
That's why I was. Oh, okay.

00:19:05.120 --> 00:19:08.240
I got you. Well, you can't, it's not like you can have five infielders, so.

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:12.260
No, no, it's true. But they also don't specify like, oh, this guy plays shortstop.

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:13.900
Yeah. This guy plays, you know what I mean?

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:18.220
It's almost like when you're first picking, you want to just be like, well, shit, I'm just

00:19:18.220 --> 00:19:20.140
going to pick the guys with the best batting averages.

00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:24.600
Yeah. Exactly. But then you realize like, oh, you need to add a catcher.

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:26.520
You need to add an outfielder.

00:19:26.620 --> 00:19:28.800
And they're on separate screens, which is fine.

00:19:29.100 --> 00:19:30.740
Yeah. Helps sort everything out.

00:19:31.760 --> 00:19:36.220
But do you think this was maybe just too much information overall for a game that's

00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:37.100
this simple?

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:40.620
I think, yeah. I was going to say, I think in general, it's really cool that you have the option.

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:44.580
It gives you that immersion and it lets you feel like, oh, this is like my career mode,

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:46.700
even though you're only playing one game and you're picking and whatever.

00:19:46.940 --> 00:19:49.720
But yeah, I do think it's like, like you said, it's so simple.

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:52.500
I'm curious as to like what difference it really makes.

00:19:52.820 --> 00:19:55.980
And I don't know if I need it in this, to be honest.

00:19:55.980 --> 00:20:01.220
I think in like, if we were one step, one level higher of like, maybe if we're on the

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:05.740
Super Nintendo, we had a little more, it felt a little more real or maybe even like the N64

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.280
or something, then I'd be like, okay, I want a little more control here.

00:20:08.280 --> 00:20:10.380
I just want to like play an arcade game of baseball.

00:20:10.820 --> 00:20:17.860
Yeah. I think that something that we'll see and we have seen throughout the evolution of sports

00:20:17.860 --> 00:20:22.000
games is just that they're all grasping for authenticity.

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:26.620
And like, what else can I add to this game to make it more authentic?

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:28.400
And this is kind of the first step.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:29.520
You've got the real teams.

00:20:29.680 --> 00:20:32.000
You've got the real players and their stats.

00:20:32.320 --> 00:20:34.060
Yeah. You don't, you can't play a season.

00:20:34.240 --> 00:20:35.800
Yeah. You can't play a series.

00:20:36.660 --> 00:20:39.880
Yeah. There really isn't any difference in what they look like, but it's a step.

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:44.640
And I think that in the history of sports games, it's an important step.

00:20:44.780 --> 00:20:45.860
And let's not forget that.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:48.160
Like that's the selling point on the back of the box.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:52.840
Like Sean's definitely right here because every single thing in bold on the back of

00:20:52.840 --> 00:20:56.100
the box is about like you basically being the manager.

00:20:56.400 --> 00:21:00.040
Like forget about, you know, like even playing the game.

00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:05.060
The whole idea is that like you're in control of your favorite team, which is an important

00:21:05.060 --> 00:21:11.100
selling point. In double dribble, you're in control of two random basketball players that you have no

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:11.920
affinity for.

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:14.620
I think you got the full, don't you have the full team?

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:16.440
In double dribble? Yeah.

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:18.960
I thought it was, I was picturing like NBA Jam for a second.

00:21:19.420 --> 00:21:21.080
Yeah. Anyway.

00:21:21.420 --> 00:21:22.720
Is it four on four or what is it?

00:21:22.760 --> 00:21:23.800
I think it's five on five. Okay.

00:21:23.920 --> 00:21:25.040
Wow. Even in double dribble.

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:26.340
Yeah. That's pretty cool. All right.

00:21:26.460 --> 00:21:28.380
Well, I like NBA Jam for what it's worth.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:29.460
I love NBA Jam.

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:33.940
It's not a knock on only having two players, but again, NBA Jam has the whole like licenses

00:21:33.940 --> 00:21:35.420
thing going on. So that helps.

00:21:35.740 --> 00:21:41.880
So you pick all your, you pick your team and then the computer like blazingly fast picks

00:21:41.880 --> 00:21:43.360
its team. I don't know why I need to show you.

00:21:43.520 --> 00:21:44.800
I can just show me the screen.

00:21:44.800 --> 00:21:46.800
Right. Afterwards, like give me the line of card.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:50.860
I'll be fine. And then you're like greeted with the stadium and stuff like that.

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:56.560
And I don't know about you guys, but this was, this was just not that great of a presentation

00:21:56.560 --> 00:22:02.860
for the stadium. It's like a billion flies have just shown up to the stadium.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:04.240
They're like blinking lights.

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:06.000
There's no like little people or anything like that.

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:08.800
There's blinking lights and there's just so much like chatter.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:15.060
And then one guy who's like whistling just over and over and over again.

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:19.920
And it's like, I think on the baseball episode, we complained about how quiet it was, but I'm

00:22:19.920 --> 00:22:23.860
thinking now that I would rather that than like this.

00:22:23.860 --> 00:22:24.720
Yeah. This like.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:29.380
It's another, it's another attempt at, at emulating that atmosphere.

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:31.520
And again, they don't have the tools for it.

00:22:31.520 --> 00:22:34.140
It's funny though, because they kind of brag about it in the manual.

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:37.320
I don't know if you guys read this, but like in the objective of the game at the end of

00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:41.020
this paragraph where I was explaining everything, it says, even hear the roar of the crowd as

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:44.280
you hit a home run. That roar of the crowd is literally just static.

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:45.940
Like it is actually really annoying.

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:50.500
And I just thought it was funny that they like kind of like bragging about a little bit.

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:52.500
And it's just like the most generic, like.

00:22:53.840 --> 00:22:55.040
I may have been impressed.

00:22:55.160 --> 00:22:56.380
Who knows? Maybe you were.

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:58.240
Yeah. I think you were.

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:03.040
But the first thing you might do, you know, you're either batting or pitching, but I think

00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:04.500
we could talk about batting first.

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:10.180
The most noticeable changes to me, just going from baseball to here is that you now have

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:14.900
the ability to move around in the, you can move from like left to right.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:19.180
In the batter's box. You could do that in baseball.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:22.860
Could you? Yeah. But I think.

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:26.640
You can adjust like where you are attempting to hit it.

00:23:26.760 --> 00:23:27.680
I think my problem. Yeah.

00:23:27.740 --> 00:23:32.640
My problem with the baseball one was that if the ball like landed in your zone where you

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:35.100
moved too far in, it just went right through you.

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:38.780
Which just didn't, it didn't help with the feeling of it.

00:23:38.820 --> 00:23:40.280
That's part of why it was haunted. Yes.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:44.780
And in this one, you can also bunt or fake bunt.

00:23:44.900 --> 00:23:49.360
I do. I don't know if the computer ever like pitched differently because it thought I was bunting,

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:53.700
but I imagine that it could work if I faked a bunt and then went around for a full swing.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:56.860
Also, I should mention too, when you move in the batter's box, I think you can only move

00:23:56.860 --> 00:23:57.840
forward and back.

00:23:58.000 --> 00:23:59.040
That's true. Yeah.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:01.520
You can't move in all four directions.

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:08.940
And then if you have runners on base, even while you're batting, you are technically also

00:24:08.940 --> 00:24:12.520
the manager at the same time and you're allowed to make calls for runners to steal.

00:24:12.640 --> 00:24:15.160
Yeah. Which is a really cool feature.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:17.800
Well, yeah. I think like, I don't know.

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:19.440
The AI.

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:20.500
We're going to get into that.

00:24:20.500 --> 00:24:26.320
Yeah. It's this uncomfortableness of like, does it know exactly what I'm doing or like, is there

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:28.720
an opportunity for mistakes and stuff like that?

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:31.700
Right. So, let's keep talking about batting for the time being.

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:37.000
How do you guys feel about hitting a ball and controlling that kind of, you know, against

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:42.780
a pitcher? Sure. I thought it was, I thought it was good, but sometimes I felt like I was a little cheated.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.420
Maybe I was just bad at it, but there were a few times where I was like, well, wait a minute.

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:48.600
I think I hit the ball there.

00:24:50.680 --> 00:24:52.620
I thought it was way too easy.

00:24:52.760 --> 00:24:58.520
And this is coming from like my, my explanation of baseball one.

00:24:58.700 --> 00:24:59.940
I'm just going to keep saying baseball one.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:01.740
This is a completely different game.

00:25:01.740 --> 00:25:04.680
Yeah. Was that it, it made me rage quit.

00:25:04.840 --> 00:25:08.160
Like I couldn't complete a game and because I couldn't hit.

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:15.960
And this game is so easy that I think I racked up like 50 hits in a game and I was leading

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:20.060
like 30, 35 to 11 at one point.

00:25:20.100 --> 00:25:21.800
It was just way too easy.

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:26.680
Yeah. I think the scores in general in this game just get out of control regardless of.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:30.480
For both teams. Regardless, computer or you.

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:33.900
There's just like home runs are very frequent.

00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:40.020
Yeah. And I would say that yes, the pitching is easy provided that they don't throw one of those

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:41.720
like insane fastballs.

00:25:42.020 --> 00:25:44.900
Yeah. That was just like, well, I'm just never going to be prepared for that.

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.580
And maybe that's more real than anything is how fast the fastballs are.

00:25:47.580 --> 00:25:50.400
There were a few that were kind of, that were slow and like right down the middle.

00:25:50.780 --> 00:25:53.220
And I just feel like I hit the ball.

00:25:54.100 --> 00:25:56.720
But like it's not, you can control like the height of your swing.

00:25:56.920 --> 00:26:01.560
So like there's not, maybe my timing was slightly off, but like visually I hit the ball.

00:26:01.560 --> 00:26:02.460
But I didn't.

00:26:02.640 --> 00:26:04.240
So maybe my timing was off.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:09.540
Maybe one of the reasons that I did well enough is because there is such a discrepancy between

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:18.100
how fast their fastball is and how slow the changeup is that they also just decided to

00:26:18.100 --> 00:26:19.600
let you swing multiple times.

00:26:20.500 --> 00:26:24.920
So if you just like time the pitch, you can hit that fastball.

00:26:25.040 --> 00:26:27.180
And if it's not a fastball, you can just swing again.

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:31.280
It's funny that they don't even count the first one as a strike against you.

00:26:31.400 --> 00:26:32.960
Like, okay, fine. You hit the ball.

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:34.520
But that first one, that's a strike.

00:26:34.860 --> 00:26:36.340
That reminds me of like baseball bugs.

00:26:36.360 --> 00:26:37.160
Wait, so how does that even work?

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:38.620
When does it count as a strike?

00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:40.280
Like when it crosses the plane?

00:26:40.900 --> 00:26:42.580
When? Only when it's crossing the plane.

00:26:42.580 --> 00:26:46.780
Only when it's officially like out of these opportunity zone.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.500
You know, like once it's been caught by the catcher, which is the same thing as passing

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:58.320
the zone. I think that this is actually the section though, batting specifically, that made me question

00:26:58.320 --> 00:27:01.820
why I built together a lineup in the first place.

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:04.840
Because averages don't seem to matter.

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:07.920
Home runs counts barely matter.

00:27:07.920 --> 00:27:12.240
Anyone is possible of hitting the ball provided you make contact with the baseball.

00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:16.280
And it doesn't matter. It's not like they hit it better or they run faster.

00:27:16.580 --> 00:27:20.720
So like there was just a huge, I feel like forget about all those stats.

00:27:20.920 --> 00:27:23.440
Like I get that that helps me think about what player it was.

00:27:23.820 --> 00:27:28.740
Forget about all those stats and just give me like out of a score of four, like their

00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:31.140
power, their contact, and their speed.

00:27:31.160 --> 00:27:32.240
And that would have been enough.

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:37.360
I mean, it's entirely possible that every time you hit a single or what would have been

00:27:37.360 --> 00:27:41.520
a single, it was a worse player.

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:43.580
But I didn't notice that specifically.

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:46.000
I didn't either. I was looking for something like that.

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:51.180
Yeah. And I had no problem whether my guy was hitting 270 or 320.

00:27:51.740 --> 00:27:55.300
Yeah. You know, like neither one was an objectively better player than the other one.

00:27:55.380 --> 00:27:57.480
That's true. And the home run goes the same too.

00:27:57.760 --> 00:27:59.720
I had a guy who. Was your pitcher hitting home runs?

00:27:59.860 --> 00:28:01.760
Yeah. I don't know if my pitcher hit a home run.

00:28:01.940 --> 00:28:06.200
I don't remember. But like you had guys who had 34 home runs and guys who had three.

00:28:06.200 --> 00:28:09.400
Three. And the guy who had three also hit a home run in that game.

00:28:09.580 --> 00:28:11.000
It's not like. It's just not like.

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:13.680
Not that it should be impossible for any player to hit a home run.

00:28:13.940 --> 00:28:17.220
Right. It's just like in the odds of my game, he just happens to hit a home run.

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:18.480
And so does the other guy.

00:28:18.660 --> 00:28:20.260
Like it just seems easy.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:25.580
And speaking of home runs, when you hit a home run, they just keep running out there.

00:28:25.880 --> 00:28:27.600
Yeah. You just sit and wait.

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:31.020
And like. Yeah. They're like, no, I'm going to get it.

00:28:31.220 --> 00:28:32.760
Yeah. And I'm worried that they might.

00:28:32.960 --> 00:28:34.160
They might just. Yeah.

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:35.860
They might like hop the fence.

00:28:36.660 --> 00:28:40.600
So I thought that was funny that like if you hit a home run that they just keep running.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:44.160
Maybe they can't see. Because when you're in the field, sometimes you can't see where you're running because

00:28:44.160 --> 00:28:45.560
the camera doesn't let you see.

00:28:45.760 --> 00:28:47.300
So maybe they have the same problem.

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:49.020
Yeah. Maybe they do have that problem.

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:52.780
And then while we're on this whole subject of your at bats and stuff.

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:59.880
Did you guys like find it frustrating to control like, okay, I hit a pretty good ball there,

00:29:00.020 --> 00:29:06.140
but it's in play. And I think this could be a triple, but I'm having trouble communicating like move to the

00:29:06.140 --> 00:29:10.620
next bag. So, yeah, I did have trouble with that at first.

00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:13.120
First, I did get used to it.

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:20.380
But then I just realized that it really doesn't matter if it's on the cusp or not, because

00:29:20.380 --> 00:29:23.080
you, they will not try and catch you.

00:29:23.580 --> 00:29:27.900
Like they will, they will cut you off so that you can't hit second if it's a double or you

00:29:27.900 --> 00:29:29.020
can't hit third if it's a triple.

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:34.100
But they will make no attempt to throw it back to the previous base and catch you in

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:35.920
a hop-off. Yeah. I noticed that too.

00:29:36.140 --> 00:29:38.960
So, yeah, you just, as long as you know how to return.

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:40.080
Yeah. Then you're fine.

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:43.080
But I just, it just like gets a little confusing sometimes.

00:29:43.140 --> 00:29:44.000
Like, wait, hold this.

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:49.300
The button combinations are weird because you have to hit a directional button and A, if

00:29:49.300 --> 00:29:53.060
you're, if you're going forward and B, if you're returning or the opposite.

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:56.820
But once you just know A, you know, A is to advance, B is to return.

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:58.620
And then just the directional buttons are the bases.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:02.020
And then like, once you get like, get your head around that, it's a little more intuitive,

00:30:02.100 --> 00:30:03.680
but it did take me a little while to get the.

00:30:03.740 --> 00:30:10.140
I also just found myself still like aggressively tapping like A and the direction at all times

00:30:10.140 --> 00:30:12.220
just, just to make sure it was going to register.

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:18.040
Same. Yeah. I think you have to hit the first base, like whatever the, the first base you hit is,

00:30:18.180 --> 00:30:19.960
and then you can input for the next one.

00:30:20.220 --> 00:30:21.100
Got it. Okay. Yeah.

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:27.600
But. Yeah. I almost wish you could just like, or, or input things in advance, you know, like a manager

00:30:27.600 --> 00:30:29.700
would make that call right away anyway.

00:30:29.900 --> 00:30:34.180
But I understand that it's like, it's more of a, a programming parsing thing where it's

00:30:34.180 --> 00:30:35.540
like, Oh, but you can't go to third.

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:40.460
He's not at second yet. And that's the other thing, because it's even confusing the way I described it because the

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:43.280
directional button is not the base you want to go to.

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:47.280
It's the way you're on. That's the base that the runner you want to advance is on in case you have two runners

00:30:47.280 --> 00:30:48.760
on base. So like that gets a little confusing too.

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:50.180
It sounds like to be like, all right, like run to second.

00:30:50.340 --> 00:30:51.340
And I'm hitting the second.

00:30:51.460 --> 00:30:53.720
I'm like, no, no, I got to hit first because he's on first.

00:30:53.720 --> 00:30:55.200
And I'm telling him to run to second.

00:30:55.280 --> 00:30:57.140
Who's on first? What's on second?

00:30:57.560 --> 00:30:58.440
Uh, very good.

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:00.780
And, um, no one tried stealing.

00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:03.720
I did not. I think I did just for the heck.

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:04.640
I tried to steal too.

00:31:04.780 --> 00:31:05.820
And I did not work out.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:10.420
Yeah. I think it might be that AI issue that you guys were talking about, but.

00:31:10.620 --> 00:31:11.900
Well, it just also feels like.

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:13.040
You can't get a lead.

00:31:13.380 --> 00:31:15.400
Yeah. Exactly. You don't know when to steal.

00:31:15.660 --> 00:31:21.140
Yeah. It's not like they, um, they don't just eventually, like if they haven't thrown the ball yet, it's not

00:31:21.140 --> 00:31:24.580
like they're going to then choose to throw it to home anyway.

00:31:24.820 --> 00:31:27.500
You know, like they're, they know that the pickoff is happening.

00:31:27.760 --> 00:31:29.840
And I tried to pick off. I tried to pick off some people too.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:31.080
And like nothing changes.

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:32.180
Have you never, um.

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:33.360
Lead off or anything either.

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:40.180
Yeah. Really. So. I, I found, I tried to pick off a lot, but going back to stealing, is it possible to

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:42.260
steal while they're pitching?

00:31:42.640 --> 00:31:43.720
Like, is that. No, no.

00:31:43.920 --> 00:31:46.880
Uh, you'd have to. It has to be like while before the pitch.

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:48.840
It has to be before the pitch. Because then you're in control.

00:31:49.120 --> 00:31:51.020
Then you're in control of the batter at that point.

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:52.280
Yeah. So yeah.

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:58.640
Not, not that great. Uh, I think even just being able to lead off the bag would have, would have went away.

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:04.260
Like, uh, I think they did solve this in the N64 version of Major League Baseball where

00:32:04.260 --> 00:32:06.060
you can, uh, yeah.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:07.060
Took that long? Yeah.

00:32:07.140 --> 00:32:11.580
You can hit the, uh, C buttons on the NES in, in the direction of the base you want to

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:18.540
go to. And if you just tap it once, you go a small lead off, medium, large, and then finally the

00:32:18.540 --> 00:32:20.320
fourth tap is like, I'm stealing.

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:22.340
You know, like, they want you to commit to it.

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:25.740
But you're like setting it to steal for the pitch so you don't have to hit a button during

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:26.880
the pitch. Yeah. Exactly.

00:32:27.180 --> 00:32:29.060
But you could do it during the pitch too.

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:30.560
Oh, gotcha. You can. Yeah.

00:32:30.640 --> 00:32:32.060
In the pitch, they won't even take a lead off.

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:35.880
If you press the appropriate bag on the pitch, they'll immediately start taking off.

00:32:36.040 --> 00:32:39.140
But again, that's like 64-bit programming.

00:32:39.800 --> 00:32:42.360
Not really 64-bit programming, but you get the idea.

00:32:42.360 --> 00:32:46.100
Yeah. There's only one other thing you can do, though, after you bat.

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:47.440
And that's pitch.

00:32:47.720 --> 00:32:52.200
Uh, it becomes the second half of the inning or the next inning, depending on if you're

00:32:52.200 --> 00:32:54.540
the home or away team. And now you must pitch.

00:32:55.020 --> 00:33:03.260
Uh, pitching here, more intuitive and also the realistic side of the game, uh, for what

00:33:03.260 --> 00:33:04.660
it's worth in terms of like-

00:33:04.660 --> 00:33:07.100
Yeah. Adding realism to actual players.

00:33:07.580 --> 00:33:12.880
Sure. Uh, and I say that because there's a staying power associated with the pitcher.

00:33:13.060 --> 00:33:14.320
Like a pitch count. Yeah.

00:33:14.480 --> 00:33:20.660
A pitch count. And also the number of fastballs you throw, which is like kind of ahead of its time to

00:33:20.660 --> 00:33:21.780
be thinking about stuff like that.

00:33:21.780 --> 00:33:27.620
Because only very recently, like in the late 2000s, did people start thinking about like,

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:29.380
uh, he's got a hundred pitches.

00:33:29.420 --> 00:33:31.560
He's not, he's going to be pulled out of the game soon.

00:33:31.680 --> 00:33:34.040
Like, you know, it's like it never was like that before.

00:33:34.040 --> 00:33:37.040
And starting pitchers would go for a very long time in games.

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:43.240
And I think they maybe realized like, I could make more money if I lasted longer in my career.

00:33:43.240 --> 00:33:45.760
So I shouldn't be pitching for so long for these teams.

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:48.080
So pitching, how do we feel?

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:49.980
I mean, I think it was fine.

00:33:50.120 --> 00:33:51.300
You had, you had, you had four pitches.

00:33:51.460 --> 00:33:56.160
What do you get? A fastball, a slow ball, a curve ball.

00:33:56.220 --> 00:33:59.680
I mean, I think you get two pitches and you can do whatever you want with them.

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.540
I would say it's three pitches, right?

00:34:01.540 --> 00:34:06.840
It's like, it's three pitches, meaning that, uh, you, you can press to make it slow.

00:34:07.220 --> 00:34:12.100
You can press upwards to make it fast and you can hold in the, like just throw a regular

00:34:12.100 --> 00:34:13.440
to just throw it down the line.

00:34:13.540 --> 00:34:16.760
But then once you throw it, you're in control kind of of the curve.

00:34:16.860 --> 00:34:19.160
You can make it a curve ball if you wanted to.

00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:21.520
So I thought it was right.

00:34:21.640 --> 00:34:23.280
It was curveball in the manual too.

00:34:23.340 --> 00:34:24.260
It was just what I did the whole time.

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:26.160
Right. It was curveball and left is screwball.

00:34:27.240 --> 00:34:31.080
Well, I mean, I guess technically, but what it's really.

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:32.440
Are you actually controlling the level?

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:36.780
You were controlling the movement of the ball.

00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:42.720
I would just hold the button I wanted to throw and it would just curve ridiculously into.

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:45.040
Like you can actually make it dance in the air.

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:46.440
And I didn't know that.

00:34:46.520 --> 00:34:47.940
I never even took my finger off.

00:34:48.300 --> 00:34:52.900
That's funny. I was able to get the computer to, to just like think it was going to be a strike, a

00:34:52.900 --> 00:34:54.520
ball. And then it actually is a strike.

00:34:54.640 --> 00:34:56.700
So what about like, but I was really bad at that too.

00:34:56.760 --> 00:34:57.920
What about like fastball? Yeah. Can you like.

00:34:58.020 --> 00:35:00.780
Just hold down. I'll, yeah, I'll explain it.

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:03.700
Uh, it's funny because I, I know what you're talking about in the manual.

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:07.060
They don't say it so clearly, but what they mean.

00:35:07.060 --> 00:35:08.380
I thought you were just selecting your pitch.

00:35:08.540 --> 00:35:15.280
Right. What they mean to say is that before you pitch, you can choose between up and down on the control

00:35:15.280 --> 00:35:17.280
pad or no button at all.

00:35:17.960 --> 00:35:20.740
Once you pitch up and down mean nothing anymore.

00:35:20.860 --> 00:35:26.580
You can't slow the speed of the ball, but left and right now come into play for direction.

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:31.820
So you can, you can bring it inside to, you know, close to the batter or you can pitch

00:35:31.820 --> 00:35:36.980
it away. If you are holding left or right while you're pitching, it will be an inside or an outside

00:35:36.980 --> 00:35:44.260
pitch. So there is some like pre, pre touch, I guess, but then there's that after touch.

00:35:44.460 --> 00:35:45.460
Right. Yeah. Okay.

00:35:45.560 --> 00:35:48.620
Yeah. So that makes it a little, a little more complicated than I thought it was.

00:35:48.680 --> 00:35:51.020
I thought it was just very like, here are your four pitches.

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:52.460
No. Yeah. You use these.

00:35:52.900 --> 00:35:56.360
And you guys mentioned that you tried picking off players, but that never worked.

00:35:56.560 --> 00:36:03.040
Never worked. Nope. I feel like the picking off the way that, that it's described in the manual implies that

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:05.120
you can pick people off from the base they're currently on.

00:36:05.240 --> 00:36:08.820
Yeah. But I think it's only ever meant to be used for if they try to steal.

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:11.320
I think that's only going to happen if you're playing a two player game.

00:36:11.700 --> 00:36:13.900
Right. Which I mean is good, right?

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:18.400
You wouldn't not want that option, but it never comes into play with the AI.

00:36:18.540 --> 00:36:20.140
Yeah. So it's, it's not really worth it.

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:25.480
And let's say you pitch a fastball down the middle and the computer makes contact with

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:32.620
it. It goes in the air and suddenly a brand new concept is introduced to baseball games on

00:36:32.620 --> 00:36:34.220
the NES. Fielding.

00:36:35.300 --> 00:36:36.360
What a concept.

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:38.180
Joe, you want to take away fielding for us?

00:36:38.180 --> 00:36:43.040
Yeah. I want to start with my, my, the first thing that happened to me that, that kind of haunted

00:36:43.040 --> 00:36:45.820
me the rest of the game is that there's a pop fly in the outfield.

00:36:46.180 --> 00:36:50.760
You can't see your outfielder, but you, you see the ball, like it's where it's going.

00:36:51.220 --> 00:36:55.000
So I just have to kind of blindly like kind of guess where the, where the outfielder is

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.340
and you're still controlling, you're still controlling him.

00:36:57.480 --> 00:37:01.240
And then finally, when it comes into view and you realize you're like 40 miles west of

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:02.780
where you're trying to be, you got to try and run the ball.

00:37:02.880 --> 00:37:06.180
You'll never make it. Uh, so that was like a very, a little bit of a frustrating thing.

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:07.740
Um, I don't know what the solution is.

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:12.680
I guess just. I mean, I guess a point like a cursor pointing towards you.

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:13.720
Yeah. Something like that.

00:37:13.900 --> 00:37:19.180
I, yeah, I, I agree, uh, with Sean there that even, even a cursor pointing towards you

00:37:19.180 --> 00:37:24.080
or maybe, um, some kind of like variation of scope.

00:37:24.200 --> 00:37:27.660
So this way you always see your player on the field.

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:33.200
But I took it to mean like where that ball was currently hit, you were never going to

00:37:33.200 --> 00:37:34.640
catch it. You know what I mean?

00:37:34.660 --> 00:37:37.360
Like, it was like, this field is big and you just weren't there.

00:37:37.460 --> 00:37:39.440
And now you have to like chase after it.

00:37:39.540 --> 00:37:44.180
Yeah, you could have tracked it. If you were in real life in the outfield, you could have easily, like you, sometimes I'm

00:37:44.180 --> 00:37:45.560
like a few, like a few feet away from it.

00:37:45.560 --> 00:37:47.360
Well, yeah, everything in this game feels like it's a line drive.

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:51.060
You know, there's no pop-up where the ball is hanging for like 10 seconds.

00:37:51.060 --> 00:37:52.160
I thought there were a lot of those.

00:37:52.300 --> 00:37:54.100
There were a few of those. There's been quite a bit.

00:37:54.340 --> 00:37:55.180
I mean, not 10 seconds.

00:37:55.260 --> 00:37:57.880
Literally. I'm just saying like, yeah, that's how long they really are.

00:37:58.280 --> 00:37:59.940
You know, like, they're up there for a while.

00:37:59.940 --> 00:38:04.580
But enough time to definitely think like any, any major league baseball outfielder would

00:38:04.580 --> 00:38:05.520
have been able to get under it.

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:09.540
I feel like maybe I just sucked as a pitcher, but for the most part, I was either getting

00:38:09.540 --> 00:38:12.280
ground balls or I was getting line drives.

00:38:12.640 --> 00:38:16.060
I never felt like I got a true, like, ooooh.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:24.440
I mean, I think even what you did there, that sound, was shorter than the amount of time.

00:38:24.580 --> 00:38:26.760
And yeah, it happens in my head as I'm playing, but.

00:38:26.900 --> 00:38:30.960
Got it. I mean, it even happens sometimes on fouls.

00:38:31.060 --> 00:38:34.260
It'll go right behind you and you can, you can get people out that way.

00:38:34.820 --> 00:38:38.180
Yeah. Are we willing to admit though that fielding in general is just a little fidgety?

00:38:38.420 --> 00:38:40.260
Like. It's entirely fidgety.

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:42.580
Yeah. Sometimes also like you are direct.

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:45.980
I'm just still talking about pop-ups, but you are directly under the ball.

00:38:46.060 --> 00:38:48.840
And it just kind of like goes through you a little bit and like lands.

00:38:49.040 --> 00:38:50.200
You weren't like perfectly there.

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:51.440
It's like, there's no way to know.

00:38:51.660 --> 00:38:58.380
Right. We all seem to talk about after you recover said ball, the speed at which you're throwing.

00:38:58.620 --> 00:39:00.700
It's like the slowest the ball moves in the whole game.

00:39:01.100 --> 00:39:06.760
I don't know what the, what the logic is behind it, but like, let's say you're, it's an outfielder,

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:08.980
specifically an AI outfielder.

00:39:09.520 --> 00:39:14.920
That ball will be on fire coming into, coming into the base that they're trying to get you

00:39:14.920 --> 00:39:21.800
out at. But if it's a, if it's like from third to first or second to first, it's moving slower than

00:39:22.340 --> 00:39:23.140
anything can.

00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:26.540
Yeah. It's almost like it's taking like the same amount of distance or like different amounts

00:39:26.540 --> 00:39:27.940
of distance over the same amount of time.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:33.080
Yeah. Like no matter what. I'm not really sure why, because it made double plays almost impossible.

00:39:33.460 --> 00:39:43.520
Yeah. And another thing about the AI, they're running AI is a little, they're a little too conservative

00:39:43.520 --> 00:39:44.920
because. Oh yeah. They don't take a chance.

00:39:45.120 --> 00:39:53.540
They don't take any chance. If you are 300 feet away and you pick up, you pick up a fly ball and somebody's at second

00:39:53.540 --> 00:39:56.100
or somebody's at third, they won't try and go home.

00:39:56.620 --> 00:39:59.240
Like if you're, if you're in control, you can do that.

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:01.180
Right. But they just stand still.

00:40:01.640 --> 00:40:02.440
It's so weird.

00:40:02.720 --> 00:40:08.700
I also found that unless they are pretty well committed, if you throw the ball to second,

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:10.640
they stay at first.

00:40:10.820 --> 00:40:13.660
They never even, they, like, even if there was like a chance maybe, right?

00:40:13.740 --> 00:40:14.720
Because you can always head back.

00:40:15.080 --> 00:40:18.800
There was never like a, like, okay, I'm going to go for, oh, he threw it there.

00:40:18.940 --> 00:40:23.540
All right. I'm going back. It's like, they just wait and see how everything is going to be played out.

00:40:23.780 --> 00:40:25.680
And you know, that's, that's a fault of the game.

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:28.320
Like there probably should have been at some difficulty sliders.

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:31.460
There's a few misses on the AI to make it feel realistic.

00:40:31.600 --> 00:40:38.060
What I did like about the fielding was that there is also an option to tag a player at

00:40:38.060 --> 00:40:40.840
the base as well by holding B instead of A.

00:40:41.020 --> 00:40:42.720
And that could have easily just went like

00:40:42.720 --> 00:40:44.040
miss, like you have to throw.

00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:48.820
But I appreciate that they thought about the infield with that in mind and had a whole

00:40:48.820 --> 00:40:53.520
second button dedicated to like figuring out ways to also tag out the, the runner.

00:40:53.520 --> 00:40:57.140
But there's also like a version of this game that I think could have existed where you

00:40:57.140 --> 00:40:59.260
couldn't get them out by just running in front of them.

00:40:59.260 --> 00:41:02.800
But you can do that too if you just make contact.

00:41:03.340 --> 00:41:08.440
Yeah. Double plays, well, maybe impossible from like the outfield.

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:11.720
They can happen if they're hit appropriately on the infield.

00:41:11.780 --> 00:41:13.220
So I like that.

00:41:13.400 --> 00:41:16.360
I like a lot about the fielding for the first time we're seeing it.

00:41:16.480 --> 00:41:20.320
You know, maybe I'm like treating it like the original baseball in the NES here.

00:41:20.320 --> 00:41:24.680
Yeah. I mean, it's, it's, I guess it's still training reels for this kind of game.

00:41:24.800 --> 00:41:30.660
But I thought it was like, I thought it was, I thought it was better than, better than

00:41:30.660 --> 00:41:32.400
expected for a first go around.

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:37.840
I'd say pitching for me was the best part because you're able to control the ball throughout

00:41:37.840 --> 00:41:38.940
the entirety of its pitch.

00:41:39.920 --> 00:41:41.560
And batting just felt too easy.

00:41:41.560 --> 00:41:42.800
So I didn't really enjoy that.

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:49.600
Uh, it's almost like you want to, you want to just get out at a certain point to help

00:41:49.600 --> 00:41:52.040
progress the game because it's going to be nine innings regardless.

00:41:52.360 --> 00:41:53.540
I felt that too a little bit.

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:58.380
And, uh, what's up with the umpires just kind of like teleporting to the scene of the

00:41:58.380 --> 00:42:04.120
crime, uh, whenever like there's an out or something, uh, they just like show up and

00:42:04.120 --> 00:42:04.980
they make the call.

00:42:05.020 --> 00:42:11.720
But what a lot of people won't notice is, is that, uh, they will change the, their like

00:42:11.720 --> 00:42:14.760
number of hands in the air based on the call as well.

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:20.420
So if it's like safe, they do the two as if they were like crossing their hands.

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:23.060
They don't do that. They just start to go wide.

00:42:23.080 --> 00:42:25.480
They're still the same amount of hands that they're going to be involved in that motion.

00:42:25.500 --> 00:42:27.120
And then if he's out, they just show the one hand.

00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:31.420
And I thought that that was like a nice touch, you know, we could use more of that in the

00:42:31.420 --> 00:42:36.500
game in general. Why not show some players sliding, even if there's not a button for it, right?

00:42:36.800 --> 00:42:40.020
Why not just occasionally a player slides to the base?

00:42:40.460 --> 00:42:41.660
Yeah, it's true.

00:42:41.860 --> 00:42:45.220
I mean, again, yeah, just that presentation and authenticity sort of thing, especially

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:46.980
for those pop-ups that you're talking about, right?

00:42:47.100 --> 00:42:51.380
Where like all of a sudden you're like, I'm standing under the ball, but it goes like kind

00:42:51.380 --> 00:42:52.440
of just behind me, right?

00:42:52.680 --> 00:42:54.920
Maybe that's a great opportunity for like a glove.

00:42:55.140 --> 00:42:57.320
Yeah. I got like an overly sized glove.

00:42:57.460 --> 00:42:58.540
Like a big old glove.

00:42:58.920 --> 00:43:01.660
Given the cartoony nature of the game anyway, to just like show up.

00:43:01.780 --> 00:43:05.280
And then this way you have a better idea of like, oh, I'm in that target zone.

00:43:05.280 --> 00:43:09.740
I will say maybe this is something that just happens in two player games because there's

00:43:09.740 --> 00:43:12.680
more that there are more opportunities for fault.

00:43:12.820 --> 00:43:17.220
But I never saw an error, even though there's a box for it.

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:20.380
And the back of the back of the back of the back of the instruction manual says that there

00:43:20.380 --> 00:43:21.220
are errors.

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:29.760
And I've definitely seen what should be errors by the AI, like just fielders running away

00:43:29.760 --> 00:43:31.960
with the ball or like running away from the ball.

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:35.420
But it would never show up on the box score.

00:43:35.560 --> 00:43:36.900
I don't really know why.

00:43:37.040 --> 00:43:38.180
I just wanted to point that out.

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:44.500
Yeah. I'm looking into it now and I can't find any evidence of what an error would be in this

00:43:44.500 --> 00:43:46.700
game. Like no one's going to drop a ball.

00:43:46.700 --> 00:43:52.780
Once it's been registered as in their possession, there's no chance for it to be like, no, actually

00:43:52.780 --> 00:43:55.460
this was the one in 96 that they dropped.

00:43:55.640 --> 00:43:56.720
You know, this just doesn't happen.

00:43:56.980 --> 00:44:02.980
So I'm not sure, but I can understand like it's in every box score ever in baseball.

00:44:03.160 --> 00:44:06.160
So might as well include it here to not pretend like they don't exist.

00:44:06.300 --> 00:44:09.760
Yeah. It's not going to happen in this particular game.

00:44:09.760 --> 00:44:11.520
They do advertise it happening though.

00:44:11.960 --> 00:44:12.780
They have a what?

00:44:14.000 --> 00:44:15.520
Oh, they have a chance of happening.

00:44:15.520 --> 00:44:17.560
They do advertise it happening.

00:44:17.780 --> 00:44:18.640
Right. Gotcha.

00:44:19.780 --> 00:44:21.540
Extra innings are possible as well.

00:44:21.640 --> 00:44:25.480
That's nice. I think in baseball it just ended.

00:44:25.600 --> 00:44:26.640
It just ended in a tie.

00:44:26.980 --> 00:44:34.720
Oh, that's right. Sam had that weird situation where he had this huge comeback and he couldn't wait to get

00:44:34.720 --> 00:44:36.360
the extras and it just ended.

00:44:36.660 --> 00:44:37.760
Yeah. He must have been playing.

00:44:38.140 --> 00:44:41.400
Not that I know anything about programming a game, but it just feels like just make more

00:44:41.400 --> 00:44:43.820
of it. Like it just makes it just let it keep going.

00:44:44.100 --> 00:44:44.920
Yeah. Is it that hard?

00:44:44.920 --> 00:44:50.080
He must have been playing in a version where like it was pre-1930s and they didn't have

00:44:50.080 --> 00:44:52.160
the lights for the night games out yet.

00:44:52.160 --> 00:44:54.780
Time to go home. So they had to just be like, yeah, we'll cancel this game.

00:44:55.680 --> 00:44:58.980
And do you guys know about the MLB challenge for this game?

00:44:59.380 --> 00:45:01.480
No. It was like a thing.

00:45:01.940 --> 00:45:03.780
It was like a Captain Commando challenge?

00:45:03.900 --> 00:45:06.840
I guess. It's not as inventive.

00:45:07.480 --> 00:45:09.640
Captain Commando challenges were a little more fun.

00:45:09.640 --> 00:45:18.880
This one is basically the only way to win a game other than winning in like runs is to

00:45:18.880 --> 00:45:22.580
exhaust all of the computer's pitchers.

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:23.580
And they just forfeit?

00:45:23.780 --> 00:45:27.420
It's the only way to complete the game without completing all nine innings.

00:45:27.420 --> 00:45:35.040
You get the computer to basically say mercy because what it proceeds to do is find an

00:45:35.040 --> 00:45:35.880
available pitcher.

00:45:36.120 --> 00:45:37.360
It opens up the menu.

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:39.640
It can't. It selects anything.

00:45:39.680 --> 00:45:44.880
And it's been and it gets told like that error sound of like, so this is not a pitcher or

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:46.480
like select a pitcher, blah, blah, blah.

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:48.200
And then it just does it again.

00:45:48.340 --> 00:45:51.580
And again, on loop until you shut off the game.

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:53.120
Oh, man. I want to do that now.

00:45:53.120 --> 00:45:54.840
So you should try to do that.

00:45:54.860 --> 00:45:55.880
Hitting is easy enough.

00:45:56.220 --> 00:46:02.500
Yeah. It definitely would happen in a nine inning game if you hit enough home runs and stuff

00:46:02.500 --> 00:46:08.400
like that. Unfortunately, it's just like, it's a lot of commitment because there's like, I think, seven

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:09.300
pitchers to go through.

00:46:09.540 --> 00:46:14.820
I was able to because I think that once like a pitcher is tired, it shows like a ball next

00:46:14.820 --> 00:46:17.600
to it or something when it goes to the select screen.

00:46:18.980 --> 00:46:24.260
But I think there was a point where I had gone through like four or five pitchers in that

00:46:24.260 --> 00:46:25.580
in that 50 hit game.

00:46:25.660 --> 00:46:28.260
That's awesome. So I think maybe it's possible.

00:46:28.440 --> 00:46:29.940
Yeah. You brought the select screen.

00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:31.300
I realize we didn't talk about that.

00:46:31.860 --> 00:46:38.660
We were we were talking about how in Ikari Warriors 2, I really liked the UI of that.

00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:40.380
Yeah. Of that select screen.

00:46:40.720 --> 00:46:44.800
This one, I wouldn't say I really like the UI, but you have to commend them for fun.

00:46:44.820 --> 00:46:48.340
Following through with the amount of available options to you.

00:46:48.640 --> 00:46:50.340
You can pitch hit. You can sub.

00:46:50.540 --> 00:46:51.940
You can put a closer in.

00:46:52.100 --> 00:46:55.840
All that shit. You can even sub position players, even though it doesn't matter at all.

00:46:55.980 --> 00:46:58.600
Right. Like now this guy is going to be playing right field.

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:00.100
Right. Like it doesn't matter.

00:47:00.440 --> 00:47:01.980
But like you can do that.

00:47:01.980 --> 00:47:05.260
And I have to applaud them for including that option.

00:47:06.200 --> 00:47:08.000
So yeah, I am.

00:47:08.840 --> 00:47:14.480
I'm amazed at how much this game wants you to think it delivers without delivering most

00:47:14.480 --> 00:47:16.260
of it. That's a good way of articulating.

00:47:16.440 --> 00:47:20.200
Because I would say I'm impressed with the amount of effort they put in and the things

00:47:20.200 --> 00:47:21.500
they thought to include.

00:47:22.380 --> 00:47:23.700
But that's all I'm impressed with.

00:47:23.840 --> 00:47:28.120
Yeah. I think we'll include this in the essential games that we're about to do.

00:47:28.340 --> 00:47:29.300
Yeah, we definitely are.

00:47:29.560 --> 00:47:37.160
Yeah. No, no. And then like, would you have been mad if as a baseball fan you got this game in 1988?

00:47:37.700 --> 00:47:38.900
Okay. All right.

00:47:39.040 --> 00:47:42.080
So let's do the essential games list.

00:47:44.540 --> 00:47:45.780
Sean, start us off.

00:47:45.960 --> 00:47:48.260
And don't forget, it's a two part question this week.

00:47:48.440 --> 00:47:51.200
It's would you have been mad if you got this in 1988?

00:47:51.560 --> 00:47:55.220
And also, is this in the essential games list?

00:47:56.220 --> 00:48:03.080
Okay. So back to our first episodes, we started off with a couple sports games.

00:48:03.220 --> 00:48:10.380
And I sort of went into the philosophy of looking at these 20, 30 years later.

00:48:10.760 --> 00:48:17.000
And I think it's important to look at the evolution of these games and how they get a

00:48:17.000 --> 00:48:21.880
little bit more in depth in terms of their presentation, their level of simulation, their

00:48:21.880 --> 00:48:24.800
authenticity. And I think this is a step in the right direction.

00:48:24.800 --> 00:48:27.680
It definitely is not without its faults.

00:48:28.760 --> 00:48:35.620
But yeah, just by virtue of being this iterative kind of game, it is not essential.

00:48:35.900 --> 00:48:43.060
But if I were a baseball fan in 1988, I would be impressed by what they showed up with.

00:48:43.240 --> 00:48:47.600
Because again, I would want to play as a team, even if it didn't mean anything.

00:48:47.600 --> 00:48:49.800
I would be impressed by the stats.

00:48:50.000 --> 00:48:55.660
I would be impressed by the relative level of minutiae that you can get into the game

00:48:55.660 --> 00:48:56.900
with pinch hitting and all that.

00:48:58.620 --> 00:49:01.620
But yeah, I mean, I'd be impressed, but not right now.

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:03.740
Got it. Joe?

00:49:04.340 --> 00:49:09.440
I wouldn't be disappointed in 1988 if as a baseball game I played this.

00:49:09.980 --> 00:49:13.640
I think it's good enough for 1988.

00:49:14.960 --> 00:49:19.260
And also there's just something, just me personally, about something about a sports game in particular

00:49:19.260 --> 00:49:21.520
where you kind of see the way it all works.

00:49:21.740 --> 00:49:23.540
It's just interesting. It's like a little machine.

00:49:23.720 --> 00:49:24.760
The way, like, this is how this works.

00:49:25.020 --> 00:49:28.060
I don't know how every video game works, but particularly with sports games, you kind

00:49:28.060 --> 00:49:30.220
of see it all functioning in a different way.

00:49:31.300 --> 00:49:38.000
But overall, it's already hard for a sports game to get onto our essential games list.

00:49:38.060 --> 00:49:43.460
And I think even as a sports game, this didn't blow me away that much anyways.

00:49:43.720 --> 00:49:46.160
Like, honestly, after a while, I was like, okay, it's baseball.

00:49:46.520 --> 00:49:48.140
Like, it's trying to do some things.

00:49:48.180 --> 00:49:50.220
It's not really delivering on all of it.

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:55.200
I do not think this is an essential game.

00:49:55.200 --> 00:50:01.340
I particularly remember as a kid playing RBI baseball, and I, in my head, remember it being

00:50:01.340 --> 00:50:04.220
better. It might not be anything close, but I'm interested to see what that's like.

00:50:04.340 --> 00:50:08.360
Maybe just all baseball games on the NES were just, like, not as good as I thought they

00:50:08.360 --> 00:50:12.280
would have been. We're nine episodes away from RBI baseball.

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:15.160
I have not played that since I was, like, eight years old.

00:50:15.320 --> 00:50:17.060
But that's it.

00:50:17.300 --> 00:50:22.260
Great. I think I said everything I needed to say here when I said that, like, I commend them

00:50:22.260 --> 00:50:25.920
for the effort, but it didn't follow through enough for it to be on the essential games

00:50:25.920 --> 00:50:27.820
list. So it's automatically off for that reason.

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:34.560
But I think if you owned this game when it came out in 1988, and some kid next door neighbor

00:50:34.560 --> 00:50:39.420
had, like, baseball on the NES because it was made by Nintendo and came in some package

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:42.620
deal, and then it comes over to your house, you're, like, the shit right now.

00:50:42.820 --> 00:50:48.880
You know? Like, it's a very competent game for what it's worth compared to the other one that we played.

00:50:48.880 --> 00:50:50.180
So I think that's worth mentioning.

00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:54.140
Another thing that's worth mentioning that I think is surprising that I've withheld until

00:50:54.140 --> 00:50:56.640
this time is that this is an LJN game.

00:50:57.260 --> 00:51:04.580
And LJN has so far released Gotcha the Sport, Jaws, The Karate Kid, and Town and Country Surf

00:51:04.580 --> 00:51:06.020
Design's Wood and Water Rage.

00:51:06.160 --> 00:51:07.200
They really like sports.

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:11.600
I mean, they've got the Gotcha the Sport.

00:51:11.820 --> 00:51:16.300
Yep. They've got the surfing and skateboarding.

00:51:16.520 --> 00:51:18.520
